# ESP32-C3 IoT Platform Rules

Layering (never skip downward):
Application → IoT Services → Communication Services → Device Services → Drivers/HAL → ESP-IDF → ESP32-C3

- Application and product profiles call platform APIs only. They must not include ESP-IDF driver headers.
- Drivers wrap ESP-IDF (`driver/*`, `esp_adc`, LEDC, GPTimer, NimBLE, `esp_wifi`, `mqtt_client`, `esp_https_ota`).
- No fake register-level hardware APIs. Document external hardware (MCP2515 CAN, RS485 transceiver, sensors).
- ESP32-C3 has no native CAN/TWAI. Automotive profiles use SPI CAN controller + transceiver.
- ISR: no blocking, no malloc, no logs. Use `xSemaphoreGiveFromISR` / `vTaskNotifyGiveFromISR` / `xQueueSendFromISR`.
- Resource locks use mutexes. Binary semaphores are for events, not mutual exclusion.
- Errors propagate as `iot_err_t`. Do not swallow `esp_err_t`.
- No production credentials in source. NVS or CI secrets only.
- Preserve working code. Do not overwrite modules without justification.
- MISRA-oriented: explicit casts, no recursion in drivers, bounded buffers, `static` internals.
- Doxygen file and function banners on every public API. Line comments for non-obvious steps.
- New modules need: `.c`, `.h`, Kconfig/config, unit test, README notes, failure scenarios.
- Product work uses Agent 10 (iot-application). Do not implement all products in one change unless asked.
- Execution order: Architect → ESP-IDF → Driver → FreeRTOS → BLE → Wi-Fi → MQTT → Security/OTA → Diagnostics → Products → Test → Performance → Docs → CI → Principal review.
